# Differential abundance {#sec-differential-abundance}

An important component of many microbiome studies is differential abundance
analysis (DAA). It is mainly used to identify differences in the abundances of
individual taxa (at any taxonomic level) between two or more groups, such as
treatment versus control groups.

The goal of DAA is thus to identify biomarkers of a certain phenotype or
condition, and gain understanding of a complex system by looking at its isolated
components. For example, the identification of a bacterial taxon that is more
abundant in healthy versus diseased individuals can lead to important
insights into the underlying mechanisms of the disease. In other words,
differentially abundant taxa can be involved in the dynamics of the disease,
which in turn helps to understand the system as a whole.

## Challenges

As discussed in @sec-stat-challenges, microbiome data display unique
properties, such as compositionality and skewed/zero-inflated distributions.
Evidently, these properties need to be addressed properly also in DAA.
Unfortunately, there is currently no consensus on how that should be done.
Instead, there exist dozens of methods employing varying techniques and,
consequently, providing highly discordant results.

The discordancy of results provided by different DAA methods was recently well
illustrated by @Nearing2022. In this study, multiple DAA methods were applied to
38 different datasets and their results were compared to one another. Similar
findings were also made by @Yang2022 who evaluated DAA methods 106 real datasets
(along with simulated data). They found especially that on many datasets almost
any taxon was detected by at least one DAA method (see Figure 7 in their
article). They thus raised concerns of potential cherry-picking, that is, a
researcher would be likely to find the taxa in favor of their hypothesis by just
trying out several different DAA methods.

## Recommendations {#sec-daa-recommendations}

Usually, when a new DAA method is introduced, the authors demonstrate it to
perform better compared to some of the existing alternatives. That is often
achieved by employing simulated data and possibly one or more real datasets.
The authors may have, however, selected their simulation models,
example datasets, evaluation metrics, compared methods, etc. specifically to
show the introduced method in a favorable light. Assessing the performance of
different methods based solely on the studies where they were originally
introduced may therefore not be advisable.

A more neutral evaluation of the performance of different DAA methods can be
found from several independent benchmarking studies (e.g. @Weiss2017,
@Hawinkel2019, @Calgaro2020, @Wallen2021, @Nearing2022, @Yang2022,
@Cappellato2022, @Roche2022, @Swift2023, @Wirbel2024, @Pelto2025,
@Gamboa-Tuz2025). Unfortunately, these studies have ended up with very
discordant conclusions on the methods. However, if we look specifically at some
benchmarking studies that have used only real datasets (instead of simulated
data), we can see that analysis of relative abundances (TSS normalized counts)
with a simple non-parametric method or linear regression has
performed well.

- @Nearing2022 studied the performance of DAA methods using 38 datasets. They
evaluated the number of detected taxa (ASVs), consistency of the detected taxa
between methods, replication of results between studies and error rate on
datasets with randomly assigned groups. They emphasized conservatism
(low error rate) in their recommendations and ended up recommending
`r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("ALDEx2")` [@Fernandes2014] and ANCOM [@Mandal2015].
In this book, we use the newer ANCOM-BC method [@ancombc2020] from the
`r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("ANCOMBC")` package instead of the original ANCOM.
@Nearing2022 also recommended
`r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("Maaslin2")` [@Mallick2021] (i.e. analyzing arcsine
square root transformed relative abundances with linear regression) as a more
powerful option.

- In addition to simulations, @Yang2022 estimated the FDR of different DAA
methods on 106 real datasets with randomly created groups. They found that,
the observed FDR was at the nominal level when, for instance, analyzing
relative abundances either with the Wilcoxon test [@Wilcoxon1945] or with
linear regression (`r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("Maaslin2")`).

-	@Pelto2025 studied how the results from different DAA methods replicate
between random partitions of datasets and between datasets from independent
studies. They found that when considering consistency along with sensitivity,
the best performance was obtained by analyzing relative abundances with a
non-parametric method or (after log transformation) with linear
regression/t-test (`r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("Maaslin2")`) or analyzing
presence/absence of taxa with logistic regression. Their approach is
illustrated in @fig-daa-replicability.

-	@Gamboa-Tuz2025 employed three real datasets to benchmark different DAA
methods against biological ground truth. They found that methods employing the
TSS normalization (with e.g. the Wilcoxon test) and RNA-seq-derived methods
performed best.

![Assessing the replicability of differential abundance analysis. (a) DAA is
performed separately on an exploratory and a validation dataset, and the
estimates for each taxon are compared. A taxon whose estimates point in
opposite directions is a conflicting finding, and a taxon whose estimates
agree is a replicated finding. The exploratory and
validation datasets are obtained (b) by randomly splitting one dataset or
(c) from two independent studies. Reproduced from @Pelto2025 under CC BY 4.0.](figures/daa_replicability.jpg){#fig-daa-replicability width="80%"
fig-alt="Schematic in three panels. Panel a: an exploratory and a validation dataset, each with a control and a case group, are analysed separately; the forest plots of five taxa are then overlaid, showing taxon 1 with conflicting estimates and taxon 4 with replicated estimates. Panel b: one original dataset is randomly split into exploratory and validation sets. Panel c: two separate studies serve as the exploratory and validation sets."}

We therefore recommend performing DAA by analyzing
**relative (TSS normalized) abundances by a non-parametric method**
(e.g., the Wilcoxon test or ordinal regression model) or, after a log
transformation, with **t-test/linear regression**. In addition to their
good performance on real data they have the benefit of being **intuitive and
easy to understand**. Linear regression of log-transformed relative abundances
is implemented, for instance, in `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("LimROTS")`
[@Anwar2025] and `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("Maaslin2")` [@Mallick2021], which we
demonstrate below (@sec-daa).

We note, however, that the performance of DAA methods likely depends
on the characteristics of the analyzed data [@Weiss2017; @Calgaro2020].
Consequently, while the methods recommended above seem to be good general
default methods, they may not necessarily be the optimal methods in many cases.
If one wants to evaluate which method may perform best specifically on their
data, one option to use the `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("benchdamic")` package
by @Calgaro2022 (see vignette
[here](https://www.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/vignettes/benchdamic/inst/doc/intro.html)).

Lastly, based on recent studies on classification accuracy of healthy vs.
non-healthy patients, it seems that a high proportion of information in
microbiome data is included in the mere **presence/absence** of taxa
[@Giliberti2022; @Karwowska2025]. A viable, and even simpler, alternative to DAA
is thus **differential prevalence analysis** (see also @Pelto2025), which is
covered in @sec-differential-prevalence.

## In practice {#sec-daa}

Following the recommendations above, we show in this section how to perform DAA
by analyzing (log transformed) relative abundances with linear regression
models, using `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("LimROTS")` and
`r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("Maaslin2")`. We first compare two groups with LimROTS
while controlling for a covariate (@sec-daa-limrots). We then show with
MaAsLin2 the simplest case where two groups are compared without other
covariates, followed by more complex analyses, namely, the analysis with
additional covariate(s) included and the analysis with interaction of two
variables. Furthermore, we illustrate the differential
analysis of positive (non-zero) abundances. Differential prevalence analysis is
shown in @sec-differential-prevalence.

### Preparing the data {#sec-prepare-data}

We use the Tengeler2020 data set [@Tengeler2020] ([@sec-data-tengeler2020]),
in which mice were humanized with the gut microbiota of either individuals
with ADHD (N = 13) or healthy controls (N = 14). The samples belong to three
different cohorts.

```{r}
#| label: load_packages_and_data

# Load the needed packages
library(mia)
library(knitr)
library(dplyr)

# Import dataset
data("Tengeler2020", package = "mia")
tse <- Tengeler2020

# Show patient status by cohort
table(tse$patient_status, tse$cohort) |>
    kable()
```

Before starting the analysis, we agglomerate the features by genus and filter
them by a prevalence threshold of 10%.
Furthermore, we create assays for relative abundances and
presence/absence of taxa. Lastly, we transform variable `patient_status` to a
factor with `Control` as the reference level. (It is a standard practice to use
the control group as the reference group in many statistical analyses.)

```{r}
#| label: prepare_data

# Agglomerate by genus and subset by prevalence
tse <- agglomerateByRank(tse, rank = "Genus")
tse <- addPrevalence(tse)
tse <- subsetBy(tse, rows = prevalence > 0.1)

# Transform count data and create assay of relative abundances
tse <- transformAssay(tse, assay.type = "counts", method = "relabundance")

# Transform count data and create assay of presence/absence of taxa
tse <- transformAssay(tse, assay.type = "counts", method = "pa")

# Transform patient_status to a factor and set "Control" as the reference level
tse$patient_status <- factor(tse$patient_status, levels = c("Control", "ADHD"))

# We also transform cohort as a factor
tse$cohort <- factor(tse$cohort, levels = c("Cohort_1", "Cohort_2", "Cohort_3"))
```

### LimROTS {#sec-daa-limrots}

We first perform DAA with `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("LimROTS")` [@Anwar2025],
which fits a linear model for each taxon. It combines the linear model and the
empirical Bayes variance moderation of `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("limma")`
[@Ritchie2015] with a reproducibility-optimized test statistic. The LimROTS
method was inspired by @Elo2008, and added support for covariates. The
statistic is tuned with bootstrap resampling so that the ranking of the taxa
is as reproducible as possible, and the p-values are obtained by permutation.
LimROTS works directly with
`SummarizedExperiment` objects, including `TreeSE`, and it adds the results to
the `rowData` of the data. LimROTS applies to log-transformed omics data.

Compared with standard linear models, such as those fitted by MaAsLin2
(@sec-daa-basic), LimROTS has some potential advantages. The empirical Bayes
moderation borrows information across taxa to stabilize the variance
estimates, which helps when there are few samples. The reproducibility
optimization adapts the test statistic to the data at hand, and the
permutation-based p-values do not rely on the distributional assumptions of
the linear model, which log-transformed abundances with many zeros often
violate [@Anwar2025].

LimROTS expects log-transformed data. We analyze log~2~ transformed relative
abundances, and the zeros are replaced by half of the smallest non-zero
relative abundance in the data before the transformation. We compare the ADHD
and control groups while controlling for `cohort` as a covariate (the role of
covariates is discussed in @sec-daa-cov). The group variable must be a factor,
and LimROTS reports the difference between its first and second level. To
obtain the log~2~-fold change of ADHD versus Control, we therefore put `ADHD`
first. The number of
bootstrap and permutation iterations is set with `niter`, and the runs can be
parallelized with the `BPPARAM` argument.

```{r}
#| label: run_daa_limrots
#| message: false

library(LimROTS)
library(BiocParallel)

# Log2-transform relative abundances; zeros are replaced by half of the
# smallest non-zero relative abundance
tse <- transformAssay(
    tse,
    assay.type = "relabundance",
    method = "log2",
    pseudocount = TRUE,
    name = "log2_relabundance"
)

# LimROTS reports the first level minus the second level of the group
tse_limrots <- tse
tse_limrots$patient_status <- factor(
    tse_limrots$patient_status,
    levels = c("ADHD", "Control")
)

set.seed(123)
tse_limrots <- LimROTS(
    tse_limrots,
    assay.type = "log2_relabundance",
    meta.info = c("patient_status", "cohort"), # Variables used in the model
    group = "patient_status", # The variable of interest
    formula.str = "~ 0 + patient_status + cohort",
    niter = 1000, # Bootstrap and permutation iterations
    BPPARAM = SerialParam(), # For example, MulticoreParam(4) to parallelize
    verbose = FALSE
)
```

The results are stored in the `rowData`. We extract the log~2~-fold change
estimates (`corrected.logfc`), the permutation-based p-values (`pvalue`) and
the Benjamini-Hochberg adjusted p-values (`BH.pvalue`), that is, the q-values
[@Benjamini1995]. Positive log~2~-fold changes (`coef`) indicate a higher
abundance in the ADHD group.

```{r}
#| label: results_daa_limrots

res_limrots <- data.frame(
    feature = rownames(tse_limrots),
    coef = rowData(tse_limrots)$corrected.logfc,
    pval = rowData(tse_limrots)$pvalue,
    qval = rowData(tse_limrots)$BH.pvalue
)

# Print the results for taxa with the smallest p-values
res_limrots |>
    arrange(pval) |>
    head() |>
    kable()
```

Using the standard criterion for statistical significance, q-value < 0.05, the
abundances of several genera differ between the groups. For instance,
_Faecalibacterium_ has a positive log~2~-fold change, so it is more abundant
in the ADHD group, while _Erysipelatoclostridium_ is less abundant. We repeat
the analysis with MaAsLin2 in the following sections and compare the results
in @sec-daa-cov.

### MaAsLin2 {#sec-daa-maaslin2}

We then perform DAA with the `Maaslin2()` function from the
`r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("Maaslin2")` package [@Mallick2021]. We show the
simplest case where two groups are compared, the analysis with a covariate,
and the analysis with an interaction of two variables.

#### Two groups {#sec-daa-basic}

Here, we perform the simplest version of DAA, namely, the comparison of two
groups without any other covariates included in the analysis.
In our case, this means comparing the relative abundances of genera between the
microbiome of ADHD individuals versus control.

The `Maaslin2()` function

- transforms counts to relative abundances,
- replaces zeros by half of the smallest non-zero relative abundance,
(separately for each taxon),
- applies log~2~ transformation to the relative abundances, and
- runs a linear regression model for each taxon using the transformed relative
abundances as the response variable.

To run the function we need to provide it with the count matrix and metadata as
inputs. We also need to provide it with the variable indicating the compared
groups, i.e. `patient_status` in our case. Furthermore, we need to specify the
folder for some output files. Lastly, we prevent `Maaslin2()` to create certain
plots. (Note that `Maaslin2()` prints a lot of some output
which is suppressed here for convenience.)

```{r}
#| label: run_daa_basic
#| results: hide

library(Maaslin2)

obj_daa_basic <- Maaslin2(
    input_data = assay(tse), # The count matrix
    input_metadata = colData(tse) |> as.data.frame(), # The metadata
    fixed_effects = "patient_status", # The predictor variable

    output = "output", # The name of the folder for output files.
    plot_heatmap = FALSE,
    plot_scatter = FALSE
)
```

Next, we extract the `data.frame` of results from the list object returned by
`Maaslin2()`. We also calculate the 95% confidence intervals and transform the
results to a slightly more convenient format. (The sample size `N` is used as
an approximation for the residual degrees of freedom (`df`) in the calculation
of confidence intervals.)

```{r}
#| label: results_daa_basic

# Extract the results from the list object and calculate the 95% CIs
res_daa_basic <- obj_daa_basic$results |>
    mutate(
        ci_lwr = coef - qt(.975, df = N) * stderr,
        ci_upr = coef + qt(.975, df = N) * stderr
    ) |>
    select(feature, name, coef, pval, qval, ci_lwr, ci_upr)

# Print the results for taxa with the smallest p-values
res_daa_basic |>
    arrange(pval) |>
    head() |>
    kable()
```

The columns of `res_daa_basic` are as follows:

- `feature` indicates the name of a taxon (here genus).
- `name` indicates the predictor variable. As `patient_status` is a
categorical variable, it also tells the group used as the _non_-reference level
(here `ADHD` as `Control` was set as the reference level).
- `coef` is the log~2~-fold change estimate, a standard way to express effect
size in DAA.
Most importantly, positive values indicate abundance being higher in the
non-reference group (here `ADHD`). More precisely, `2 ^ coef` would be
interpreted as the ratio of geometric means of the relative abundances
between the ADHD and Control groups.
- `pval` is the unadjusted p-value.
- `qval` (q-value) is the FDR adjusted p-value [@Benjamini1995]. This is
generally used to define statistical significance. Typically, q-values < .05
are considered (statistically) significant.
- `ci_lwr` and `ci_upr` are the lower and upper bounds of the 95% confidence
intervals for the log~2~-fold change.

Now, if we use the standard criterion for statistical significance, namely,
q-value < 0.05, we can see that the DAA result is statistically significant for
the genera named as _[Ruminococcus] gauvreauii group_, _Faecalibacterium_,
_Catabacter_, _[Clostridium] innocuum group_ and _Erysipelatoclostridium_.
(`r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("Maaslin2")` has some problems with special characters
such as `[` or `]`.)
Moreover, for instance, the positive log~2~-fold change estimate of
_Faecalibacterium_ tells that the average relative abundance of
_Faecalibacterium_ is higher in the ADHD group.

#### Visualizing the results {#sec-daa-viz}

While the numbers above provide summarized information about the group
differences for each taxon, it is often useful to further examine these
associations in more detail. That can be done, for instance, using strip
plots with boxplots. We show here how such plots can be created for the
significant taxa by using `plotBoxplot` function from
the `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("miaViz")` package (@fig-daa-boxplots).

```{r}
#| label: fig-daa-boxplots
#| fig-cap: "Relative abundances of five significant genera in the ADHD and Control groups of the Tengeler2020 data, shown as strip plots with boxplots in a separate facet for each genus. The bars at the bottom show the prevalence of each genus in each group."
#| fig-alt: "Five facets, one per genus, each with free y-axis scales and showing relative abundance in the ADHD and Control groups as boxplots overlaid with points for individual samples, plus horizontal prevalence bars below the boxes."

library(miaViz)

# Create strip plots with boxplots (and prevalence bars)
plotBoxplot(
    tse,
    features = c(
        "[Ruminococcus]_gauvreauii_group",
        "Faecalibacterium",
        "Catabacter",
        "Erysipelatoclostridium",
        "[Clostridium]_innocuum_group"
    ),
    assay.type = "relabundance", # To plot relative abundances
    x = "patient_status", # The variable on the x (horizontal) axis
    facet.by = "rownames", # To create a separate facet for each feature
    scales = "free_y", # To allow different y-axis scales
    add.proportion = TRUE, # Add bars indicating prevalence
    point.offset = "quasirandom", # To plot points nicely
    point.size = 2, # The size of the points
    point.color = "black", # The color of the edges of the points
    point.alpha = 1, # The transparency of the edges of the points
    box.alpha = .20
) + # The transparency of the boxplots
    theme(strip.text = element_text(size = 7)) # The size of the facet labels
```

In each facet,

- the thicker horizontal line in each boxplot indicates the median,
- each box indicates the interquartile range (IQR), namely, the range between
the 25th (Q1) and 75th percentile (Q3),
- the lower whisker indicates the smallest value above $Q1 - 1.5 \times IQR$,
and
- the upper whisker indicates the largest value below $Q3 + 1.5 \times IQR$.
- The horizontal bars at the bottom indicate the prevalence of each genus in
each group.

The strip plots and boxplots clearly show, for instance, how the relative
abundance of _Faecalibacterium_ is higher in the ADHD group, as was indicated
by the positive the log~2~-fold estimate (`coef`) in the results table above.
Moreover, for instance, the prevalence bars of _Erysipelatoclostridium_
show how _Erysipelatoclostridium_ is more prevalent in the control group.

Lastly, we show how the results for all genera can be summarized visually
by plotting the log~2~-fold estimates and their 95% confidence
intervals (@fig-daa-forest).

```{r}
#| label: fig-daa-forest
#| fig-cap: "Forest plot of the log2-fold change estimates (ADHD vs. Control) and their 95% confidence intervals for all genera in the Tengeler2020 data, ordered by the estimate."
#| fig-alt: "Forest plot with one row per genus, sorted by estimate, showing the log2-fold change as a point with a horizontal 95% confidence interval."
#| fig-height: 9
# Plot the log2-fold changes and the 95% confidence intervals
plotForest(
    res_daa_basic,
    effect.var = "coef",
    ci.lower.var = "ci_lwr",
    ci.upper.var = "ci_upr",
    id.var = "feature",
    order.by = "coef"
)
```

#### With covariates {#sec-daa-cov}

Here we show how to perform DAA with an additional covariate included in the
analysis. One usually wants to include a variable as a covariate if they suspect
that it may act as a _confounding_ variable, namely, a variable that causes a
spurious association between the variable of main interest and the abundances.

We use here `cohort` as a covariate. We proceed as above except that we now set
`fixed_effects = c("patient_status", "cohort")`. Furthermore, we now calculate
the q-values manually so that they are calculated separately for each variable
in the model (indicated by the variable `metadata` in `obj_daa_cov$results`).
This is because `Maaslin2()` calculates the q-values over all
variables in the model which is not what here as we are only interested in the
variable `patient_status`.

```{r}
#| label: daa_cov
#| results: hide

# Run Maaslin2 with patient_status and cohort as predictor variables
obj_daa_cov <- Maaslin2(
    input_data = assay(tse),
    input_metadata = colData(tse) |> as.data.frame(),
    fixed_effects = c("patient_status", "cohort"),
    output = "output",
    plot_heatmap = FALSE,
    plot_scatter = FALSE
)

# Extract the results and calculate the 95% CIs and q-values
res_daa_cov <- obj_daa_cov$results |>
    mutate(
        ci_lwr = coef - qt(.975, df = N) * stderr,
        ci_upr = coef + qt(.975, df = N) * stderr
    ) |>
    group_by(metadata) |>
    mutate(qval = p.adjust(pval, method = "fdr")) |>
    ungroup() |>
    select(feature, name, coef, pval, qval, ci_lwr, ci_upr)
```

```{r}
#| label: print_daa_cov_1

# Print a sample of the results
res_daa_cov |>
    arrange(feature, name) |>
    head() |>
    kable()
```

Note that there now appear results also for the variable `cohort`. As `cohort`
consists of three groups, namely, `Cohort_1` (the reference group), `Cohort_2`
and `Cohort_3`, there are results separately for the comparison between
`Cohort_2` vs. `Cohort_1` (`cohortCohort_2`) and for the comparison between
`Cohort_3` vs. `Cohort_1` (`cohortCohort_3`).

We can now examine how the inclusion of `cohort` as a covariate affects the
results for `patient_status`.

```{r}
#| label: print_daa_cov_2

# Print the results for taxa with the smallest p-values for patient_status
res_daa_cov |>
    filter(name == "patient_statusADHD") |>
    arrange(pval) |>
    head() |>
    kable()
```

When comparing these results to the results in @sec-daa-basic, we
can see that, for instance, the q-values of _Faecalibacterium_ and
_Catabacter_ are now somewhat smaller. Nevertheless, the results look overall
rather similar to the results without the covariate included. Therefore, cohort
does not seem to be a confounder causing spurious associations between
patient status and the abundances of any taxa.

These results also agree well with those of LimROTS with the same model
(@sec-daa-limrots). The same genera have the smallest p-values, and their
log~2~-fold changes have the same signs. The estimates are not identical, as
the zeros are replaced differently (MaAsLin2 uses the smallest non-zero value
of each taxon, whereas we used the smallest value in the whole data with
LimROTS) and the p-values are computed differently. In this example, LimROTS
gave somewhat smaller q-values, so a few more genera passed the q < 0.05
threshold.

#### With an interaction {#sec-daa-ia}

Here we show a more complex version of DAA, namely, a case where the interaction
effect of two variables is analyzed. We give this example here for illustration
purposes but note that such complex analysis may not be advisable in a small
dataset such as our example dataset.

We analyze the interaction between `patient_status` and `cohort`.
We proceed as in @sec-daa-cov above except that we now add also the interaction
term into the formula. As `Maaslin2()` does not directly support the analysis
with interactions we must first create the interaction dummy variables in to the
`tse` object. The main effects `patient_status` and `cohort` as well as the
interaction dummy variables are then included in the `Maaslin2()` function.

```{r}
#| label: run_daa_ia
#| results: hide

# Create dummy variables for the interaction term
tse$patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_2 <-
    ifelse(tse$patient_status == "ADHD" & tse$cohort == "Cohort_2", 1, 0)
tse$patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_3 <-
    ifelse(tse$patient_status == "ADHD" & tse$cohort == "Cohort_3", 1, 0)

# Run Maaslin2 with patient_status, cohort and their interaction terms
obj_daa_ia <- Maaslin2(
    input_data = assay(tse),
    input_metadata = colData(tse) |> as.data.frame(),
    fixed_effects = c(
        "patient_status",
        "cohort",
        "patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_2",
        "patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_3"
    ),
    output = "output",
    plot_heatmap = FALSE,
    plot_scatter = FALSE
)

# Extract the results and calculate the 95% CIs and q-values
res_daa_ia <- obj_daa_ia$results |>
    mutate(
        ci_lwr = coef - qt(.975, df = N) * stderr,
        ci_upr = coef + qt(.975, df = N) * stderr,
        metadata = ifelse(
            metadata %in% c(
                "patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_2",
                "patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_3"
            ),
            "patient_status:cohort",
            metadata
        )
    ) |>
    group_by(metadata) |>
    mutate(qval = p.adjust(pval, method = "fdr")) |>
    ungroup() |>
    select(feature, name, coef, pval, qval, ci_lwr, ci_upr)
```

```{r}
#| label: run_daa_ia_1

# Print a sample of the results
res_daa_ia |>
    arrange(feature, name) |>
    head() |>
    kable()
```

The results object `res_daa_ia` now also includes the interaction
terms `patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_2` and
`patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_3`.

We examine the term `patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_3`. It indicates how the
differences between the ADHD and Control groups vary between cohorts 1 and 3.

```{r}
#| label: print_daa_ia_2

# Print the results for the interaction term patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_3
res_daa_ia |>
    filter(name == "patient_statusADHD_cohortCohort_3") |>
    arrange(pval) |>
    head() |>
    kable()
```

The smallest p-value is observed for _Coprobacter_. As the corresponding
log~2~-fold change
estimate is negative it means that the difference in the relative abundance of
_Coprobacter_ between the ADHD and Control groups is higher in Cohort 1 than
in Cohort 3.

This interaction can be better illustrated by plotting the relative
abundances of _Coprobacter_ by `patient_status` and `cohort`
(@fig-daa-interaction).

```{r}
#| label: fig-daa-interaction
#| fig-cap: "Relative abundances of Coprobacter in the ADHD and Control groups of the Tengeler2020 data, shown separately for each cohort. The bars at the bottom show the prevalence of Coprobacter in each group."
#| fig-alt: "Facets for each cohort with the same y-axis scale, each showing Coprobacter relative abundance in the ADHD and Control groups as boxplots with points for individual samples and prevalence bars below. ADHD is higher in Cohort 1 and Control is higher in Cohort 3."

# Plot the abundances of Coprobacter by patient_status and cohort
plotBoxplot(
    tse,
    features = c("Coprobacter"),
    assay.type = "relabundance", # To plot relative abundances
    x = "patient_status", # The variable on the x (horizontal) axis
    facet.by = "cohort", # To create a separate plot for each cohort
    scales = "fixed", # To have the same y-axis scale in each facet
    add.proportion = TRUE, # Add bars indicating prevalence
    point.offset = "quasirandom", # To plot points nicely
    point.size = 2, # The size of the points
    point.color = "black", # The color of the edges of the points
    point.alpha = 1, # The transparency of the edges of the points
    box.alpha = .20
) # The transparency of the boxplots
```

Here we can clearly see how the relative abundances of _Coprobacter_ are higher
in the ADHD group in Cohort 1 but they are higher in the Control group in
Cohort 3. As the association is positive in Cohort 1 (the baseline cohort) and
negative in Cohort 3, sign of interaction estimate is negative (the association
becomes more negative when we move from the baseline cohort to a non-baseline
cohort).

Note that a similar plot could also be used to illustrate a possible confounding
effect.

### Positive abundances {#sec-daa-pos}

It may sometimes be biologically relevant to analyze only the positive
(non-zero) relative abundances of taxa. We thus briefly show here how to
perform such analysis using the `maaslin3()` function from the
`r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("maaslin3")` package [@Nickols2024]. Purely for
illustration purposes, we show here the analysis with an interaction term.

We proceed as in the previous sections. `maaslin3()` fits both a model for the
positive abundances and a logistic regression model for the presence/absence
of the taxa (the latter is used in @sec-DPA). Here we extract the results of
the former, `fit_data_abundance`, from the list object returned by
`maaslin3()`. We also
need to set `median_comparison_abundance = FALSE` in the function call as we
want to estimate the differences of (positive) relative abundances and not make
any compositional adjustments.

```{r}
#| label: daa_pos_ia

library(maaslin3)

# Run maaslin3 function.
obj_daa_pos_ia <- maaslin3(
    tse,
    output = "output",
    formula = ~ patient_status * cohort,
    median_comparison_abundance = FALSE,
    verbosity = "ERROR",
    plot_associations = FALSE,
    plot_summary_plot = FALSE
)

# Extract the results and calculate the 95% confidence intervals and q-values
res_daa_pos_ia <- obj_daa_pos_ia$fit_data_abundance$results |>
    filter(!is.na(pval_individual) & N_not_zero > 6) |>
    mutate(
        ci_lwr = coef - qt(.975, df = N_not_zero - 6) * stderr,
        ci_upr = coef + qt(.975, df = N_not_zero - 6) * stderr
    ) |>
    group_by(metadata) |>
    mutate(qval = p.adjust(pval_individual, method = "fdr")) |>
    ungroup() |>
    select(feature, name, coef,
        pval = pval_individual, qval, ci_lwr, ci_upr,
        N_not_zero
    )

# The results for the interaction between patient_status and cohort (when
# comparing Cohort_3 to Cohort_1) for the taxa with the smallest p-values
res_daa_pos_ia |>
    filter(name == "patient_statusADHD:cohortCohort_3") |>
    arrange(pval) |>
    head() |>
    kable()
```

The output is similar to the outputs in the previous sections. It, however,
now includes also `N_not_zero` indicating the number of samples with
positive (non-zero) relative abundances, that is, the sample size
used in the analysis for each taxon.

The results are interpreted in a similar manner as in normal DAA
(@sec-daa-ia). For instance, the estimates (`coef`) indicate the log~2~-fold
changes of positive relative abundances (as `maaslin3()` performs log~2~
transformation to the positive relative abundances).

### Alternative options {#sec-daa-alternative}

#### Advanced compositionality adjustments

We have shown above (sections -@sec-daa-basic, -@sec-daa-cov, -@sec-daa-ia and
-@sec-daa-pos) how to perform DAA by analyzing relative abundances
(TSS normalized counts) without any advanced adjustments for compositionality.
As explained in @sec-daa-recommendations, this choice was motivated by
some recent benchmark studies that have employed only real data, especially the
study by @Gamboa-Tuz2025.

We note, however, that in some benchmark studies such simple versions of DAA
have performed worse to some more advanced methods when compared on
_simulated_ data with strong compositional effects (e.g. @Roche2022, @Yang2022).
We therefore give below some options for incorporating more advanced
compositionality adjustments in DAA.

- In `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("Maaslin2")`, the default normalization method (TSS)
can be replaced by TMM normalization [@Robinson2010] by including
`NORM = "TMM"` in the `Maaslin2()` function.
- In the analysis of positive abundances with `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("maaslin3")`,
the adjustment for compositional bias can be enabled by setting
`median_comparison_abundance = TRUE` in `maaslin3()` function.
- One can use DAA methods that are specifically designed to account for
the compositionality of microbiome data. Such methods include, for instance,
  - `r BiocStyle::Biocpkg("ANCOMBC")` [@ancombc2020],
  [vignette](https://www.bioconductor.org/packages/devel/bioc/vignettes/ANCOMBC/inst/doc/ANCOMBC.html).
Note that we refer here to the older version of ANCOM-BC. There is also a
newer version (ANCOM-BC2) available, but some problems have been observed in its
performance (e.g., @Pelto2025).
  - `LinDA` [@Zhou2022] from the `r BiocStyle::CRANpkg("MicrobiomeStat")` package,
  [reference manual](https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/MicrobiomeStat/MicrobiomeStat.pdf).

#### A non-parametic alternative to MaAsLin2

As MaAsLin2 analyzes log transformed relative abundances, it needs to replace
the zeros by some positive numbers before the transformation.
If one wants to avoid this somewhat arbitrary imputation, we suggest performing
DAA by using non/semi-parametric ordinal regression models (ORM). ORM can be
seen as an extension of the familiar Wilcoxon test in the sense that it provides
p-values similar to those of the Wilcoxon test in the simple two group
comparison, but it can also incorporate covariates (and interaction terms)
[@Harrell2001; @Liu2017]. ORM has also performed well in one benchmark study
[@Pelto2025].

As there does not currently exist a good implementation of ORM for
DAA one must use a custom function. Such custom function with application
examples is given in
[extra materials](https://microbiome.github.io/OMA-extras/docs/devel/pages/extra_daa.html#sec-orm).
We also note that, in contrast to
`Maaslin2()` function, the custom function provides confidence intervals and
q-values (calculated for the variable of interest) automatically in its output.

## Exercises {#sec-differential-abundance-exercises}

We use the [`peerj13075`](data_sets.qmd#sec-data-peerj13075) dataset.

Solve the exercises in order, as each exercise continues from the previous
one. Try to solve each exercise yourself before opening the solution.

:::: {.callout-tip icon="false"}
## Exercise 1: Reading DAA results

**Goal:** Understand how the results of DAA are read.

Imagine that you have compared the relative abundances of three taxa between a
`Treatment` group and a `Control` group. `Control` was set as the reference
level. The results are the following:

| feature | coef | pval  | qval |
|---------|-----:|------:|-----:|
| Taxon A | 1.0  | 0.001 | 0.01 |
| Taxon B | -2.0 | 0.004 | 0.03 |
| Taxon C | 1.5  | 0.020 | 0.12 |

Answer the following questions without writing any code. The section
@sec-daa-basic will help you.

1. Which taxa are statistically significant when the standard criterion
q-value < 0.05 is used?

2. For which taxa is the estimated abundance higher in the `Treatment` group?

3. The log~2~-fold change (`coef`) of Taxon A is 1. What is the ratio of the
geometric means of the relative abundances between the `Treatment` and
`Control` groups? What is the ratio for Taxon B?

4. The p-value of Taxon C is smaller than 0.05. Why do we use the q-value
instead of the p-value to define statistical significance?

5. Why is it not a good idea to run several DAA methods and report the one
that gives the most significant taxa?

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Solution

1. Taxon A and Taxon B, because their q-values are smaller than 0.05.

2. Taxon A and Taxon C, because their log~2~-fold changes are positive. The
abundance is higher in the non-reference group (`Treatment`) when `coef` is
positive. Note that the difference is not statistically significant for
Taxon C.

3. The ratio is `2 ^ coef`. For Taxon A, it is $2^{1} = 2$, that is, the
geometric mean is two times higher in the `Treatment` group. For Taxon B, it
is $2^{-2} = 0.25$, that is, the geometric mean in the `Treatment` group is a
quarter of that in the `Control` group.

4. In DAA, one test is performed for each taxon. The more tests we perform,
the more likely it is that some of them give small p-values just by chance.
The q-value is a p-value adjusted for multiple testing, and it controls the
false discovery rate (FDR).

5. Different DAA methods often give very different results. If we try many
methods, we are likely to find a method that supports our hypothesis just by
chance (cherry-picking). The method should therefore be chosen before the
analysis, for example, based on the recommendations in
@sec-daa-recommendations.
:::
::::

:::: {.callout-tip icon="false"}
## Exercise 2: Prepare the data

**Goal:** Learn to prepare the data for DAA.

1. Load the `peerj13075` dataset from the `mia` package and store it in a
variable called `tse`.

2. Check how many samples there are in each group of the `Diet` column.

3. Agglomerate the data to genus level with `rank = "genus"`. (Note that
the rank names of this dataset are written in lowercase.) Then add the
prevalence and keep only the genera that are present in more than 50% of the
samples. How many genera remain?

4. Add an assay of relative abundances called `"relabundance"`.

5. Convert the `Diet` column to a factor and set `Mixed` as the reference
level.

6. Why is it important to know which group is the reference level?

**Useful functions:** `data()`, `table()`, `mia::agglomerateByRank()`,
`mia::addPrevalence()`, `mia::subsetBy()`,
`mia::transformAssay()`, `factor()`

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Solution

```{r}
#| label: exercise_daa_prepare

library(mia)

# 1. Load the data
data("peerj13075", package = "mia")
tse <- peerj13075

# 2. Number of samples in each diet group
table(tse$Diet)

# 3. Agglomerate to genus level and keep prevalent genera
tse <- agglomerateByRank(tse, rank = "genus")
tse <- addPrevalence(tse)
tse <- subsetBy(tse, rows = prevalence > 0.5)
nrow(tse)

# 4. Add relative abundances
tse <- transformAssay(tse, assay.type = "counts", method = "relabundance")

# 5. Set Mixed as the reference level
tse$Diet <- factor(tse$Diet, levels = c("Mixed", "Veg"))
```

6. The log~2~-fold changes compare the non-reference group to the reference
group. Here, a positive `coef` will mean that the abundance is higher in the
`Veg` group than in the `Mixed` group, and a negative `coef` the opposite. If
the reference level was `Veg`, the signs would be reversed.
:::
::::

:::: {.callout-tip icon="false"}
## Exercise 3: Compare two groups

**Goal:** Learn to perform a simple two-group DAA with `Maaslin2()` and to read
its results.

1. Run `Maaslin2()` to compare the genera between the diet groups. Give it the
`counts` assay as `input_data`, `colData(tse)` converted to a `data.frame` as
`input_metadata`, and `fixed_effects = "Diet"`. Set `output = "output"`,
`plot_heatmap = FALSE` and `plot_scatter = FALSE`. Store the result in a
variable called `obj_daa`.

2. Extract the `results` table from `obj_daa`. Add the lower and upper bounds
of the 95% confidence intervals as columns `ci_lwr` and `ci_upr`, as shown in
@sec-daa-basic. Keep the columns `feature`, `name`, `coef`, `pval`, `qval`,
`ci_lwr` and `ci_upr`, and store the table in a variable called `res_daa`.

3. Show the six genera with the smallest p-values.

4. How many genera have a q-value smaller than 0.05?

5. What does the value `DietVeg` in the `name` column tell? How would you read
a negative value in the `coef` column?

**Useful functions:** `Maaslin2::Maaslin2()`, `colData()`, `as.data.frame()`,
`dplyr::mutate()`, `stats::qt()`, `dplyr::select()`, `dplyr::arrange()`,
`head()`, `sum()`

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Solution

```{r}
#| label: exercise_daa_basic
#| results: hide

library(Maaslin2)
library(dplyr)

# 1. Run Maaslin2 with diet as the predictor variable
obj_daa <- Maaslin2(
    input_data = assay(tse, "counts"),
    input_metadata = colData(tse) |> as.data.frame(),
    fixed_effects = "Diet",
    output = "output",
    plot_heatmap = FALSE,
    plot_scatter = FALSE
)
```

```{r}
#| label: exercise_daa_basic_results

# 2. Extract the results and calculate the 95% CIs
res_daa <- obj_daa$results |>
    mutate(
        ci_lwr = coef - qt(.975, df = N) * stderr,
        ci_upr = coef + qt(.975, df = N) * stderr
    ) |>
    select(feature, name, coef, pval, qval, ci_lwr, ci_upr)

# 3. Genera with the smallest p-values
res_daa |>
    arrange(pval) |>
    head()

# 4. Number of genera with q-value < 0.05
sum(res_daa$qval < 0.05)
```

5. `DietVeg` tells that the predictor variable is `Diet` and that the results
compare the `Veg` group to the reference group `Mixed`. A negative log~2~-fold
change means that the estimated relative abundance of the genus is lower in
the `Veg` group than in the `Mixed` group. Whether the difference is
statistically significant is seen from the q-value.
:::
::::

:::: {.callout-tip icon="false"}
## Exercise 4: Visualize the results

**Goal:** Learn to visualize DAA results with strip plots, boxplots and a
forest plot.

1. Take the names of the three genera with the smallest p-values from
`res_daa`. Plot their relative abundances in the diet groups with
`plotBoxplot()`. Use `assay.type = "relabundance"`, `x = "Diet"`,
`facet.by = "rownames"`, `scales = "free_y"` and `add.proportion = TRUE`.

2. Plot the log~2~-fold changes and their 95% confidence intervals for all
genera with `plotForest()`. Order the genera by their log~2~-fold change.

3. How can you see from the forest plot whether the abundance of a genus is
higher or lower in the `Veg` group? How can you see whether the 95%
confidence interval contains zero?

**Useful functions:** `dplyr::arrange()`, `head()`, `dplyr::pull()`,
`miaViz::plotBoxplot()`, `miaViz::plotForest()`

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Solution

```{r}
#| label: exercise_daa_visualize

library(miaViz)

# 1. Strip plots with boxplots for the three genera with the smallest p-values
top_genera <- res_daa |>
    arrange(pval) |>
    head(3) |>
    pull(feature)

plotBoxplot(
    tse,
    features = top_genera,
    assay.type = "relabundance",
    x = "Diet",
    facet.by = "rownames",
    scales = "free_y",
    add.proportion = TRUE
)

# 2. Forest plot of the log2-fold changes and their 95% CIs
plotForest(
    res_daa,
    effect.var = "coef",
    ci.lower.var = "ci_lwr",
    ci.upper.var = "ci_upr",
    id.var = "feature",
    order.by = "coef"
)
```

3. Each point shows the log~2~-fold change of one genus, and the line shows its
95% confidence interval. Points to the right of zero mean that the abundance
is estimated to be higher in the `Veg` group, and points to the left of zero
that it is lower. If the line crosses zero, the confidence interval contains
zero, and the difference is not statistically significant at the 5% level
before adjusting for multiple testing.
:::
::::

:::: {.callout-tip icon="false"}
## Exercise 5: Add a covariate

**Goal:** Learn to include a covariate in DAA.

1. Make a cross-table of the `Diet` and `Geographical_location` columns. Are
the diet groups evenly distributed across the cities?

2. Convert the `Geographical_location` column to a factor with the levels
`Pune`, `Ahmednagar` and `Nashik`, in this order. `Pune` is then the
reference level.

3. Run `Maaslin2()` again, now with
`fixed_effects = c("Diet", "Geographical_location")`. Store the result in a
variable called `obj_daa_cov`.

4. Extract the results and calculate the q-values separately for each
variable, as shown in @sec-daa-cov. Store the table in a variable called
`res_daa_cov`.

5. Show the six genera with the smallest p-values for `DietVeg`.

6. Why can it be important to include the city as a covariate? Why are the
q-values calculated separately for each variable?

**Useful functions:** `table()`, `factor()`, `Maaslin2::Maaslin2()`,
`dplyr::group_by()`, `dplyr::mutate()`, `stats::p.adjust()`,
`dplyr::ungroup()`, `dplyr::select()`, `dplyr::filter()`,
`dplyr::arrange()`, `head()`

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Solution

```{r}
#| label: exercise_daa_covariate_prepare

# 1. Cross-table of diet and city
table(tse$Diet, tse$Geographical_location)

# 2. Set Pune as the reference level
tse$Geographical_location <- factor(
    tse$Geographical_location,
    levels = c("Pune", "Ahmednagar", "Nashik")
)
```

1. In the cross-table, the rows are the diet groups and the columns are the
cities, and each cell is the number of samples with that diet from that city.
The groups are evenly distributed if the proportions of `Mixed` and `Veg`
samples are similar in each city (column).

```{r}
#| label: exercise_daa_covariate
#| results: hide

# 3. Run Maaslin2 with diet and city as the predictor variables
obj_daa_cov <- Maaslin2(
    input_data = assay(tse, "counts"),
    input_metadata = colData(tse) |> as.data.frame(),
    fixed_effects = c("Diet", "Geographical_location"),
    output = "output",
    plot_heatmap = FALSE,
    plot_scatter = FALSE
)
```

```{r}
#| label: exercise_daa_covariate_results

# 4. Extract the results and calculate the q-values for each variable
res_daa_cov <- obj_daa_cov$results |>
    group_by(metadata) |>
    mutate(qval = p.adjust(pval, method = "fdr")) |>
    ungroup() |>
    select(feature, name, coef, pval, qval)

# 5. Genera with the smallest p-values for diet
res_daa_cov |>
    filter(name == "DietVeg") |>
    arrange(pval) |>
    head()
```

6. If the diet groups are not evenly distributed across the cities, and the
city itself is associated with the skin microbiome, the city can act as a
confounder. Differences that we see between the diet groups could then be
partly due to differences between the cities. With the city as a covariate,
the diet effect is estimated while the city is held constant. You can compare
the results to those of Exercise 3: if the estimates and q-values for diet
change a lot, the covariate affects the diet results.

`Maaslin2()` adjusts the p-values over all variables in the model. We are
mainly interested in diet, so we adjust the p-values only over the tests
for diet. This way, the tests for the city do not affect the q-values of
diet.
:::
::::

